  "description_overrides": {
    "Germinal centre - light zone": {
      "source_key": "gc_light",
      "description": "Germinal centre - light zone: shown at the capsular-facing pole. B cells capture antigen retained by follicular dendritic cells and present it to follicular helper T cells. Successful cells can recycle to the dark zone for further expansion or differentiate toward memory B cells and antibody-secreting plasma cells. Cells that fail selection undergo apoptosis and are cleared by tingible-body macrophages. Class switching is not an obligatory light-zone step; it often begins during early B-cell activation before a mature germinal centre forms. The zone boundary and cellular pathways are schematic."
    },
    "Germinal centre - dark zone": {
      "source_key": "gc_dark",
      "description": "Germinal centre - dark zone: shown at the inward-facing pole. Proliferating B cells expand and undergo AID-dependent somatic hypermutation. Cells move toward light-zone selection, and selected cells may recycle back for further proliferation. A germinal centre distinguishes a responding secondary follicle; this static specimen does not assign a fixed time after antigen exposure."
    },
    "High endothelial venules": {
      "source_key": "hev",
      "description": "High endothelial venules: representative postcapillary venules in the paracortex, lined by tall endothelial cells through which many naive lymphocytes enter the node from blood. The four section-visible venules are teaching samples; connecting capillaries and venular drainage are not fully represented. Endothelial cells and vessel calibre are enlarged for visibility."
    },
    "Medullary cords": {
      "source_key": "cords",
      "description": "Medullary cords: branching lymphoid tissue between the medullary sinuses, rich in plasma cells, macrophages and lymphocytes. Plasma cells secrete antibody that can be carried onward in lymph. Cords are tissue domains, not lymphatic lumens or direct ducts into the efferent vessel."
    },
    "Reticular fibre meshwork": {
      "source_key": "reticular",
      "description": "Reticular fibre meshwork: enlarged representative stromal strands in the subcapsular, trabecular and medullary sinuses. These are solid supporting structures within lymph-filled spaces, not open lymphatic tubes. The separate parenchymal conduit system can distribute small soluble lymph-borne material; its continuous cortical route and cellular sheath are not resolved by this sinus-only mesh."
    },
    "Afferent lymphatic vessels": {
      "source_key": "afferent",
      "description": "Afferent lymphatic vessels: six illustrative thin-walled collecting lymphatics entering through the convex capsule into the subcapsular sinus. Intraluminal valves help prevent backflow. Vessel calibre and valve geometry are enlarged for readability."
    },
    "Efferent lymphatic vessel": {
      "source_key": "efferent",
      "description": "Efferent lymphatic vessel: the illustrated hilar outlet carries lymph, soluble material including antibody, and recirculating cells toward downstream lymphatic pathways. Its valves help prevent backflow. One vessel is shown; efferent number and calibre vary."
    },
    "Lymphatic valves": {
      "source_key": "valves",
      "description": "Lymphatic valves: paired endothelial leaflets with connective-tissue cores oriented to discourage backflow. Downstream is toward the node in the afferent vessels and away from the node in the efferent vessel. The leaflets are shown statically; their closure mechanics and flow-driven movement are not simulated."
    }
  },
